model=qwen2.5:3b
Photosynthesis is the process by which plants and other organisms use sunlight to synthesize foods with the help of chlorophyll, a pigment found in their cells. During this process, carbon dioxide from the air and water absorbed through the roots are transformed into oxygen and glucose (a type of sugar) inside the leaves.

Essentially, photosynthesis is how plants convert light energy, usually from the sun, into chemical energy stored within carbohydrates like glucose. This chemical energy can then be used by the plant for growth and development, as well as other life processes such as maintaining temperature and expelling waste (oxygen).

The primary equation for photosynthesis in plants is:

\[ 6 \text{CO}_2 + 6 \text{H}_2\text{O} + \text{(light energy)} \rightarrow \text{C_6H_{12}O_6} + 6 \text{O}_2 \]

In this equation, CO₂ represents carbon dioxide and H₂O stands for water. The end products are glucose (a sugar) and oxygen.

Photosynthesis occurs in the chloroplasts of plant cells and is crucial not only to plants but also to the entire ecosystem as it produces approximately 80% of the world's oxygen.